  content: "From Robert Needham Cust, Barrister-at-Law, and Late of Her Majesty's Indian Civil Service (*A Sketch of the Modern Languages of Africa, Accompanied by a Language-Map*, Vol. I, Trübner's Oriental Series, London: Trübner & Co., Ludgate Hill; Hertford: Printed by Stephen Austin and Sons, 1883, PDF pp. 1–340 / Book pp. [i]–xvi, 1–287):\n1. **Front Matter, Provenance, Trübner's Oriental Series Catalogue & 6 Portrait Plates (PDF pp. 1–44, 257 / Book pp. [i]–xvi)**:\n   - **Provenance & Catalogue**: Digitized from the **Merensky-Biblioteek, Universiteit van Pretoria** copy (stamped `10 DEC. 1941`, Klasnommer `ZTP.2-64`, Registernommer `68959 CUST`, flyleaf price notes `\"30/-\"` and `\"2 vols 21/-\"`), preceded by the 16-page **Trübner's Oriental Series Catalogue** (dated `500—17/9/83`, listing 25 published sets and 4 works in preparation, including Cust's *Modern Languages of the East Indies* [1878, 12s.], *Linguistic and Oriental Essays* [1880, 18s.], and *Modern Languages of Africa* [2 vols., pp. xxiv–566]). Dedicated to **His Majesty Leopold II., King of the Belgians**, with Shakespeare's epigraph (`\"I speak of Africa and golden joys.\" — Ancient Pistol`) and epigraphs from Juvenal, Tasso, and Camoens.\n   - **Six Photographic Portrait Plates (31 Scholars, Explorers & Missionaries)**: **Plate I** (Lepsius, Koelle, Schön, F. Müller, Bleek, Latham); **Plate II** (J. L. Wilson, F. W. Newman, Krapf, Barth, Saker, Christaller); **Plate III** (Stanley, Moffat, Nachtigall, Grout, Steere, Ravenstein); **Plate IV** (Norris, Livingstone, Hanoteau, Faidherbe, Rebman, Reinisch); **Plate V** (Beke, Baikie, Duveyrier, Archdeacon H. Johnson of Upper Niger, Archdeacon D. Crowther of Lower Niger, Schweinfurth); and **Plate VI** (p. 212: `\"HIC NIGER EST.\" SAMUEL CROWTHER, D.D.`, *S. V. White, Photo., Reading*).\n2. **Chapters I–VII: Methodology, Classification, Polyglottism & Scripts (Book pp. 1–80)**:\n   - **Root-Naming Rule & Ethnonym Semantics (Ch. I, pp. 11–14)**: Drops Anglo-Saxon plural `-s` and Bantu class prefixes (`Swahíli, Nyika, Kamba, Pokómo`), noting that both Bantu **`abántu`** and Galla **`oromo`** literally mean **'men'** (p. 12).\n   - **East African Polyglottism (Ch. I, p. 16 & p. 123)**: **King Mtesa of Uganda** speaks 6+ languages (`Nyoro, Ganda, Swahíli, Soga, Zulu, Arabic`, plus `Galla`, p. 123); a Ma-Kua chief speaks 5; an Indian merchant at Karagwe speaks 10 African languages; Livingstone's half-caste elephant hunter speaks 12.\n   - **Six-Family Classification (Ch. IV, pp. 50–60)**: Adopts **Friedrich Müller's** 6 main divisions mapped in 6 colours by **E. G. Ravenstein** (who won a Certificate of Merit at the Venice Geographical Congress, p. xiii): **I. Semitic** (Ch. VIII, pp. 81–93, 10 languages), **II. Hamitic** (Ch. IX, pp. 94–140, 28 languages across Egyptian [1], Libyan/Berber [9], and Ethiopic [18] Sub-Groups), **III. Nuba-Fulah** (Ch. X, pp. 141–161, 17 languages: 16 Nuba [including **V. Kwáfi** & **VI. Masai**] + 1 Fulah with 5 dialects), **IV. Negro** (Ch. XI, pp. 162–287, 194 languages across Atlantic [67], Niger [38], Central [59], and Nile [31, including **XXI. Kavirondo**]), **V. Bántu** (Vol. II), and **VI. Hottentot-Bushman** (Vol. II). Notes **Krapf's** East African terms **`Nilo-Hamitic / Nilotic`** and **`Nigro-Hamitic / Nigritic`** (p. 54) and **Reinisch's** two Ethiopic-Hamitic clusters (*Cluster 1: A. Bishári, B. Saho, C. Dankálí, D. Somáli, E. Galla*; *Cluster 2: A. Bilin, B. Agau of Lasta, C. Agaumidr, D. Falásha, E. Kamant*, p. 119).\n3. **Chapter IX.C.II — Galla or Orómo (Book pp. 122–125 / PDF pp. 166–169)**:\n   - **Habitat, Boundaries & Population (pp. 122–123)**: Stretches from the frontiers of Abyssinia (**10° N. Lat.**) down to the **mouth of the Dana (Tana) River** near **Mombása** in Kenya, bounded on the East by the **Somáli** and on the West by the **Berta** and **Dinka**. A fierce and restless pastoral race of **5 or 6 millions**, pressing upon the Bántu tribes around Mombása, while the Royal Races of **U-Ganda, U-Nyoro, and Karagwe** on Victoria Nyanza belong to the **Huma** tribe of the Galla Race.\n   - **Autonyms (`Orma / Oromo` vs. `'Sons of Galla'`, p. 122)**: **D'Abbadie** notes that in war they call themselves **`'Sons of Galla'`**, while their national self-designation is **`Orma`** or **`Oromo`** (*'men'*).\n   - **The Five Chief Galla Dialects Enumerated by Krapf & Schmidt (pp. 122–123)**:\n     1. **I. North of Shoa**: Spoken largely by Mahometans.\n     2. **II. East of Shoa**: Spoken toward Somáli-Land and Dankáli-Land.\n     3. **III. South of Shoa on the River Hawash**: Described by **Schmidt** in ZDMG in his analysis of Krapf's translation of St. Matthew.\n     4. **IV. On the River Godjeb**: The dialect of **Karl & Lorenz Tutschek's** Munich Grammar and Dictionary (1844–1845).\n     5. **V. The Equatorial or South Galla (*Bararetta* / Tana River) Dialect in Kenya**: Investigated by **Rev. Thomas Wakefield** at **Ribé** near Mombása, and recorded in **Krapf's 1850 *Vocabularies of Six East-African Languages***.\n   - **Charles New on Hunter 'Helot Races' (*Wata*, pp. 122–123)**: **Rev. Charles New** states that among the Galla live **Helot Races** who live by hunting, pay tribute to the Galla, and speak the Galla language, though ethnologically distinct.\n   - **Primary Grammatical & Lexical Authorities Reviewed by Cust (pp. 123–125)**:\n     - **Rev. Dr. J. L. Krapf**: Published *Elements of the Galla Language* (1840), *Vocabulary of the Galla Language* (1842), *Vocabularies of Six Languages* (1850), and Scripture translations in the Roman alphabet (at his death in Nov. 1881, Krapf was found on his knees by his bedside with corrected proofs of his Swahíli Dictionary on his table, p. 119). Because Krapf's 1840 *Elements* was out of print and only one copy existed in the Royal Geographical Society library, **Cust had a manuscript copy made and sent to Rev. T. Wakefield at Ribé** (pp. 124–125).\n     - **Prof. F. W. Newman**: Appended a comparative grammatical note to Tutschek's Grammar demonstrating analogies with the **Libyan (Berber) Sub-Group**.\n     - **Dr. W. H. I. Bleek**: Pointed out the presence of click/ejective sounds.\n     - **Bishop G. Massaia**: Published a Latin Grammar (*Lectiones Grammaticales*, Paris, 1867) forcing Amháric (Semitic) and Oroman (Hamitic) into identical chapter sections.\n     - **Karl & Dr. Lorenz Tutschek (1838–1845, Munich)**: Compiled the 1844–1845 Dictionary and Grammar with 4 liberated African slaves in Munich.\n     - **Enaréa & Lango/Kidi (pp. 124–125)**: Notes that the Galla overran the old Christian kingdom of **Enaréa** (visited by Portuguese Jesuit **Fernandez** in 1613 and **D'Abbadie** in 1845, where coffee grows wild), and that the **Lango or Kidi** on the Somerset Nile (met by Speke, Grant, Felkin & Emin Bey) are connected with the Galla.",
